# Pressure Belts and Winds: The Engine of World Climate

> Air rises at the Equator, sinks in the subtropics, and spills between
> as the winds that steer every climate on Earth. This lesson maps the
> pressure belts, the winds between them, and the storms that ride them.

## 1. What Sets the Air in Motion

Three controls govern every wind:

- **Pressure gradient force** — air is pushed from high pressure to
  low pressure; steeper gradients mean stronger winds.
- **Coriolis effect** (Ferrel's law) — the Earth's rotation deflects
  moving air to the **right in the Northern Hemisphere** and to the
  **left in the Southern Hemisphere**.
- **Friction** — drag from the surface slows the wind and weakens the
  Coriolis deflection near the ground.

## 2. The Seven Pressure Belts

@diagram tree id="pressure-belts-tree" layout="tree" aria="World pressure belts from equator to poles"
  "Pressure belts" -> "Equatorial low (doldrums)"
  "Pressure belts" -> "Subtropical highs (horse latitudes)"
  "Pressure belts" -> "Subpolar lows"
  "Pressure belts" -> "Polar highs"
@/diagram

| Belt | Latitude | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Equatorial low | ~0–10° | Rising warm, moist air; calm, thundery doldrums |
| Subtropical highs | ~30–35° N and S | Sinking dry air; calms of the horse latitudes; site of the great deserts |
| Subpolar lows | ~60–65° N and S | Stormy convergence of westerlies and polar easterlies |
| Polar highs | 90° N and S | Sinking, intensely cold, dense air |

[note]
The belts shift about 5–10° with the overhead Sun — northward in July,
southward in January. This seasonal migration is the taproot of the
monsoons.
[/note]

## 3. Planetary Winds

| Wind | Blows from | Direction | Traits |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Trade winds | Subtropical highs to the equator | NE (Northern Hemisphere), SE (Southern) | Steadiest winds of the era of sail |
| Westerlies | Subtropical highs to subpolar lows | SW (Northern), NW (Southern) | Roaring forties and furious fifties of the southern seas |
| Polar easterlies | Polar highs to subpolar lows | Easterly both hemispheres | Cold, shallow, and dry |

## 4. Famous Local Winds

| Wind | Region | Nature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Loo | Northern plains of India | Hot, dry, gusty summer afternoons |
| Chinook | Leeward slopes of the Rockies | Warm snow-eating winter wind |
| Foehn | Leeward slopes of the Alps | Warm and dry |
| Mistral | Rhone valley, France | Bitterly cold, gusty winter drainage |
| Sirocco | Sahara to the Mediterranean | Hot, dust-laden; causes blood rain |
| Harmattan | West Africa | Dry, dusty; nicknamed the doctor |

[example]
The same physics names them all: air descending a mountain range
compresses, warms, and dries — whether it is called foehn or chinook.
[/example]

## 5. Cyclones and Anticyclones

| Feature | Tropical cyclone | Temperate (extratropical) cyclone |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Birthplace | Warm oceans, roughly 5–20° latitude | Mid-latitudes, along the polar front |
| Energy source | Latent heat of warm sea water | Contrast of warm and cold air masses |
| Structure | Calm eye ringed by storm wall; no fronts | Frontal wedges of warm and cold air |
| Weather | Storm surge, torrential rain, high winds | Widespread rain and drizzle, gusty winds |

[definition]
A **tropical cyclone** is a low-pressure storm with winds spiralling
inward and upward around a calm **eye**; the **storm surge** — sea
pushed ashore by the storm — is usually its deadliest weapon. In the
North Indian Ocean, names are assigned from lists prepared by WMO
panels of the region's countries, released by Indian forecasters.
[/definition]

An **anticyclone** is the opposite: high pressure with slow, sinking
air, giving calm, clear weather and cool nights — winter fog traps
beneath them.

## 6. Jet Streams

Jet streams are narrow bands of near-tropopause winds, hundreds of
kilometres wide but only a few thick, blowing at well over 200 km/h.

[info title="The monsoon connection"]
The **subtropical westerly jet** rides south of the Himalaya in
winter; its northward retreat in early June signals the burst of the
southwest monsoon over India.
[/info]

## 7. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Ferrel's law? | Deflection right in the Northern Hemisphere |
| Doldrums lie near? | The equatorial low |
| Horse latitudes? | Subtropical highs, ~30–35° |
| Which belt feeds the deserts? | Subtropical highs |
| Stormy 40–50°S westerlies? | Roaring forties |
| Calm core of a cyclone? | The eye |
| Winter westerly jet over India? | Subtropical westerly jet |
